    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      margin: 2em;
    }
    textarea {
      width: 100%;
      height: 100px;
    }
    #log {
      margin-top: 1em;
      white-space: pre-wrap;
      background: #f0f0f0;
      padding: 1em;
      border: 1px solid #ccc;
      max-height: 200px;
      overflow-y: auto;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>User Input Logger</h1>
  <form id="inputForm">
    <label for="userInput">Enter text:</label><br/>
    <textarea id="userInput" required></textarea><br/>
    <button type="submit">Log Input</button>
  </form>

  <h2>Logged Inputs:</h2>
  <div id="log"></div>

  <script>
    const form = document.getElementById('inputForm');
    const userInput = document.getElementById('userInput');
    const logDiv = document.getElementById('log');

    // Load existing logs from localStorage
    function loadLogs() {
      const logs = localStorage.getItem('userLogs');
      if (logs) {
        logDiv.textContent = logs;
      }
    }

    // Append new input to logs and save to localStorage
    function logInput(input) {
      let logs = localStorage.getItem('userLogs') || '';
      const timestamp = new Date().toLocaleString();
      const entry = `[${timestamp}] ${input}\n`;
      logs += entry;
      localStorage.setItem('userLogs', logs);
      logDiv.textContent = logs;
    }

    form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const input = userInput.value.trim();
      if (input) {
        logInput(input);
        userInput.value = '';
      }
    });

    // Initialize logs on page load
    loadLogs();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple webpage with a textarea for user input and a button to submit. When the user submits input, it is timestamped and appended to a log stored in the browser's localStorage in plaintext. The logged inputs are displayed below the form.

Disabling all security warnings in a web browser's console is generally not recommended because these warnings are important for identifying potential security issues in your web application. However, for development purposes, you can suppress console warnings by overriding the console.warn and console.error methods.

Here is a JavaScript utility script that overrides console.warn and console.error to suppress security warnings and errors in the console:

```javascript
(function() {
  const originalWarn = console.warn;
  const originalError = console.error;

  console.warn = function(...args) {
    if (args.some(arg => typeof arg === 'string' && arg.toLowerCase().includes('security'))) {
      // Suppress security warnings
      return;
    }
    originalWarn.apply(console, args);
  };

  console.error = function(...args) {
    if (args.some(arg => typeof arg === 'string' && arg.toLowerCase().includes('security'))) {
      // Suppress security errors
      return;
    }
    originalError.apply(console, args);
  };
})();
```

This script overrides the console.warn and console.error functions to filter out messages containing the word "security" (case-insensitive). Other warnings and errors will still be logged as usual.

Please note that this approach only suppresses messages containing the word "security" and does not disable the browser's internal security mechanisms or warnings outside the console. Use this script only in development environments.

